 "relevance_note": "Single LAD-I patient with two CD18 (ITGB2) mutant alleles: \"identified two mutant alleles in CD18 derived from a patient with partial (type II) Leu-CAM deficiency\".",
 "method_note": "Read the whole paper by hand; no table transcription (the paper has no tables). In scope: the single patient (label 'patient', male from 'his parents') and his parents, labelled 'mother of patient' and 'father of patient'. Gene is printed only as CD18, never ITGB2. No HGVS notation is printed; cdna_hgvs_as_stated and protein_hgvs_as_stated hold the legacy nucleotide and amino-acid descriptions exactly as printed (numbering: 'bp 1 = A of the first translation initiation site'; 'The initiator methionine is considered the first amino acid'). Three variants recorded for the patient: mutation A (bp 587 A>C, lys196 thr), mutation B (bp 1,777 C>T, arg593 cys) and a third, silent substitution (guanine²4 → thymine, leucine → leucine at codon 8); the silent one is recorded because the authors list it among the 'three point mutations' and do not call it common or non-causal; its zygosity and allele are not stated. Zygosity 'compound heterozygous' for A and B is the authors' hedged conclusion ('These data suggested ... compound heterozygote', followed by 'Conclusive evidence supporting these findings was independently derived'); the results also say 'the patient was a heterozygote for mutant B'. Inheritance: mutant B 'inherited maternal' because the mother's cDNA hybridized with the mutant B oligonucleotide; mutant A left 'not reported' because the father was not tested at the variant level (paternal origin is only 'suggested' by elimination). No variant row for the father for the same reason; mother's variant row is mutant B, zygosity from 'his heterozygous parents (4)'. The EBV-cell CD18 expression values ('15, 70, and 53% of normal, respectively' for 'the patient, his heterozygous parents (4) and a normal individual') give three values for four named entities, so they were not assigned to individuals. Contradictions / conversion artefacts: the abstract prints 'Lys19 → threonine' and the Figure 2 legend 'lysine¹9', while the introduction and results print 'lys196' and 'lysine196' (consistent with bp 587); the abstract speaks of 'two mutant alleles' while the results report 'three point mutations' (third is silent). No clinical details beyond 'severe and recurrent bacterial infections'; all checklist items are not reported for all three people.",
